Actually I remember: Camel added `ClassFilters` / `ObjectInputFilter` in `org.apache.camel.support.ObjectHelper`? There's `org.apache.camel.util.ObjectHelper`... Hmm.

For this training data, I don't need to replicate the exact upstream fix; I need a correct, non-bypassable whitelist defense at the right layer.

Design of the fix:

Option A: Filter at the sink (`DefaultExchangeHolderUtils.deserialize`) with a whitelist ObjectInputStream. This is the strongest single point of defense because ALL deserialization goes through this method.

Option B: Filter at the router level — but the router only sees bytes; can't validate contents without parsing. Not good.

So the fix should be in `DefaultExchangeHolderUtils`:

```java
package org.apache.camel.component.infinispan.remote.protostream;

import java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStream;
import java.io.InvalidClassException;
import java.io.ObjectInputFilter;
import java.io.ObjectInputStream;
import java.io.ObjectStreamClass;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

import org.apache.camel.support.DefaultExchangeHolder;
import org.apache.camel.util.ClassLoadingAwareObjectInputStream;

final class DefaultExchangeHolderUtils {

    private static final Set<String> ALLOWED_CLASSES = createAllowedClasses();

    private DefaultExchangeHolderUtils() {
    }

    public static DefaultExchangeHolder deserialize(byte[] bytes) throws IOException {
        try (ByteArrayInputStream bais = new ByteArrayInputStream(bytes);
             WhitelistObjectInputStream ois = new WhitelistObjectInputStream(bais)) {
            return (DefaultExchangeHolder) ois.readObject();
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            throw new IOException(e);
        }
    }

    private static Set<String> createAllowedClasses() {
        Set<String> allowed = new HashSet<>();
        allowed.add(DefaultExchangeHolder.class.getName());
        allowed.add(String.class.getName());
        allowed.add(byte[].class.getName());
        allowed.add(Number classes: Integer, Long, Double, Float, Short, Byte, Boolean, Character);
        allowed.add(java.util.HashMap, LinkedHashMap, ArrayList, ...);
        // primitives handled by JVM
        return Collections.unmodifiableSet(allowed);
